In article <2720@aplcen.apl.jhu.edu> arrom@aplcen.apl.jhu.edu
  (Ken Arromdee (600.429)) writes:
>Hold it.
>
>When you have, say, -38 AC, not only does the monster try to hit a number
>from -1 to -38, but also an amount of points equal to a random number from
>1 to 38 is subtracted from the damage the monsters do to you.

In article <30083@mirror.UUCP> mguyott@mirror.UUCP
  (Marc Guyott) writes:
>I am still not happy with this FEATURE.  I would rather have a constant armor
>class with a fixed minimum (say -10 or -20, whatever seemed reasonable).  As
>Ed pointed out, any monster should have a one in 20 chance of hitting but a
>-1 armor class is like being naked once you get into the lower levels of the
>dungeon.  I would be much happier if your armor class varied between, say,
>-15 and -38 instead of -1 and -38.  I would always expect my low armor class to
>afford me some minimal protection for every blow.

Now I understand that having an enormously negative armor class makes the
game too easy, but I do not understand the solution that was implemented.
What does having a random armor class have to do with Nethack's version
of reality?  Does your armor magically fade in and out like a ghost?  Not
only can you not count on your armor, it just doesn't make sense.  There
is already randomness introduced in how hard the monster hits (and that
does make sense).

I don't think placing a ceiling (floor?) on the armor class as someone
suggested would be a good idea, though.  After you've reached the max,
there would be no more rewards for searching for better stuff.  That
would take a little out of the game.  And changing the point at which
randomness begins from -1 to -15, as Marc suggested, would not fix this
"feature", it would just make it a little more tolerable.

    My solution is this:

Make the effective armor class (the protection the armor affords us) a
FUNCTION of the actual armor class (what is decremented by getting better
armor).  This function would be linear for large armor classes (above 0)
and then slowly decay so that each armor class improvement actually
improves one's protection by a smaller amount each time.  The important
feature here is that, while the difference between -20 and -21 Ac is a
smaller improvement than between -15 and -16, IT IS STILL AN IMPROVEMENT.
And it's something you can count on.  The adventurer is still rewarded
for getting better armor, but he can't become invincible.

  Here is my function:

    / Ac >= 0:  f(Ac) = Ac
   {
    \ Ac <  0:  f(Ac) = -x * log(1 - Ac / x)
                where x is somewhere around 20
f(x) is continuous;  f'(x) is also continuous

A graph follows:

  Here is how it converts actual Ac to effective armor class (f(Ac))
  for different x's:

               x = 10    x = 20    x = 30    x = 40
   Ac          f(Ac)     f(Ac)     f(Ac)     f(Ac)

   -1           -1        -1        -1        -1
   -5           -4        -4        -5        -5
  -10           -7        -8        -9        -9
  -20          -11       -14       -15       -16
  -40          -16       -22       -25       -28
  -80          -22       -32       -39       -44

  It looks something like this for x = 20:

                           |         ,'
                           |       ,'
                           |     ,'
                  -40 -20  |   ,'
     -100 -80 -60  |-30|-10| ,'
     __|___|___|___|_|_|_|_|'___________
                        ,,'|__ -10
                   ,,,''   |__ -20
           ,,,,,'''        |__ -30
     ''''''                |__ -40

So, as you can see, you (Nethack developers) can choose how much to
limit high effective armor class values by choosing an x that is
appropriate.  If the effective armor class can be a floating point
number, the protection vs. Ac curve will not be jumpy.

Well that's my $.02 worth, I hope you guys working on the code will
really consider this solution, I beleive it to be a good one
(of course :-).

